cartridge "BARRICADE/T.BRANTNER/1988"

' BARRICADE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Barricade" by Tony Brantner, Ahoy! April
' 1988 (issue 52; Commodore 64 machine language, BARRICADE ML at $C000,
' started by a two-line BASIC loader). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/barricade-ml.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is
' built for the ColecoVision: wooden crates on a dark warehouse floor inside
' a steel fence, a worker in a hard hat and little box robots as sprites
' that glide from cell to cell, big crate letters on the title, sounds.
'
' The game: four to eight robots hunt you across a yard full of crates.
' Push crates (a whole row of them at once, in eight directions) to build
' pens around the robots. When no robot can take a single step, the level
' is won: 100 points for each robot, plus the bonus, which runs down from
' 100 while you play. A robot that reaches you costs a life (four in all);
' each level the robots step more often.
'
' From the original: the yard (crates on every other cell, a free lane
' around them, the worker in the middle, the robots in the corners and the
' middles of the sides, in the C64's order); the worker walks in eight
' directions and pushes the row of crates in front of him when there is a
' free cell at its end (the first crate goes to that cell); the robots step
' together, each one heading for the worker (the stick direction toward
' him, then the neighbouring directions in the C64's order 0,-1,+1,-2,+2,
' -3,+3,4) onto a free cell or the worker; a robot with no free cell around
' it is trapped, and when all are trapped at once the level ends; a robot
' that steps onto the worker catches him; the board, the bonus (100) and
' the robots start again after a catch; 100 a robot, then the bonus point
' by point; 20 levels, the last one repeating; the robots' step every
' (21 - level) x 8 passes of the C64's loop (about 2.5 frames each:
' 50 frames at level 1), the worker's every 16 passes (5 frames); the bonus
' loses a point every 256 passes (81 frames); the number of robots (4-8)
' chosen on the title.
'
' Liberties: the yard is 26 x 17 cells (the C64's was 38 x 19) so that it
' fits the ColecoVision's safe columns; the worker steps every 6 frames
' and moves at once when the stick is pushed (the C64 read it every fifth
' frame); the robots never step more often than the worker (the C64's
' robots were as fast at level 19 and twice as fast at level 20: here
' level 18 on all step every 6 frames); robots and worker glide between
' cells; a trapped robot shows crossed-out eyes; the high score; the
' robots appear one by one, faster than on the C64; GAME OVER then the
' title (the C64 asked PRESS FIRE TO PLAY AGAIN and showed the robot
' menu again).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks and pushes. On the title
' the stick chooses (up/down a line, left/right the number of robots) and
' a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

